I spent the better part of a year building a homelab before I admitted what it actually was: a portfolio.

Not a homelab. Not a "lab environment." A portfolio — the kind of thing that shows I can actually build the stuff I claim to know.

## Why a blog

Every job description for a privacy engineer or compliance role I want asks for "experience." I don't have that. What I have is a homelab that touches GDPR-adjacent problems — data residency, retention, subject access requests — and the ability to document what I built and why.

So this is me building in public. The homelab will stay behind a Tailscale mesh, but the thinking gets published.

## What this is

The nth degree is about engineering depth — the kind you get from running production infrastructure, even if that production is one person's cloud-native homelab. I'm interested in:

- Compliance as engineering, not paperwork
- Infrastructure that respects the data it handles
- Building things that scale to "fifty-person SaaS" even if they never will

No list of takeaways. No conclusion that restates the intro. Just posts that try to be worth reading.

## First

If you're here from LinkedIn — hi. Drop me a message if anything here is useful or wrong. I want to know both.
